From: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
Subject: amdv: describe our own HDR10 output honestly (mastering luminance +
 primary order)
 
Two defects in the HDR10 output packet built by send_hdmi_pkt(). Both make the
box lie to the sink about what it just sent, and both only bite when the DV
core is used as a tone-mapping engine with an HDR10 wire output (VS10 HDR10) -
native passthrough is unaffected.
 
1. MASTERING LUMINANCE IS THE SOURCE'S, NOT OURS.
 
hdr_info comes back from the DM (it is the blob's output struct - no GPL code
writes it) with the source's mastering-display luminance echoed through
unchanged. Measured on S6 with hdmitx_pkt_dump, playing a 1000-nit-mastered
title through VS10 HDR10 output:
 
  DM target  400 nits -> DRM max_lum 1000, min 1, max_cll 439, max_fall 106
  DM target 2607 nits -> DRM max_lum 1000, min 1, max_cll 439, max_fall 106
  DM target 8000 nits -> DRM max_lum 1000, min 1, max_cll 439, max_fall 106
 
i.e. completely invariant across a 20x sweep of the target. So the box maps the
picture for an N-nit display and then tells the sink "mastered at 1000", and the
sink applies its own roll-off against that wrong reference - a second tone map,
with nothing anywhere in the chain compensating. Substitute the target the DM
was actually handed. Falls back to the source values when no target is known,
so any path that has not run a conversion is unchanged.
 
MaxCLL/MaxFALL are ZEROED whenever the mastering luminance is substituted.
They describe the CONTENT and the DM does not report what it produced, so after
a remap the source's figures are a known-false statement about the signal on
the wire rather than merely an unknown one - and they are the binding term on
the dominant sink rule (LG's documented HDR10 roll-off with DTM off uses
min(mastering, MaxCLL)), so leaving them silently cancels the corrected
mastering luminance. CTA-861-H 6.9.1 says a group whose data bytes are all zero
"shall" be interpreted as unknown, and footnote 19 names this exact case:
pre-processed content delivered without content-light-level information. So
zeroing is the standards-blessed way to say "remapped, no longer known" and lets
the sink fall back to the mastering luminance we do know.

THE SUBSTITUTION IS GUARDED THREE WAYS, and the zeroing only ever happens when
the substitution actually fired:

- dovi2hdr10_nomapping clear. Nothing is remapped in that mode. Note this is
  driven by FLAG_DOVI2HDR10_NOMAPPING, a debug bit that is clear by default, so
  on its own this condition gates nothing in any shipping configuration. It is
  kept because it is correct, not because it is load-bearing - the two guards
  below are what actually bound the change.
- src_format is FORMAT_DOVI or FORMAT_HDR10, i.e. a real HDR video source is
  being tone-mapped. Without this the substitution also fires for GUI-only /
  SDR-source HDR10 output (cur_src_format = FORMAT_SDR, g_dst_format =
  FORMAT_HDR10), where the DM target describes no content at all and the result
  is a GUI frame advertising "mastered at our target, content light level
  unknown". This fork has prior HDR10 GUI brightness faults; that path stays out
  of scope.
- tgt_max != max_lum, i.e. the substitution changes something. The default table
  entry is 1000 nits and 1000-nit masters are the common case - including the
  title measured above - so without this the luminance is rewritten 1000 -> 1000
  while MaxCLL/MaxFALL are wiped, moving the sink's roll-off knee (LG, DTM off,
  min(mastering, MaxCLL)) from the real 439 up to 1000 for no corrective benefit
  whatsoever. That would be strictly worse than leaving the packet alone.

KNOWN INCOMPLETENESS, deliberate: on the LEGACY (non-multi) path the helper
returns 0 for src_format >= 3, so the fix is a no-op for HLG / HDR10+ / CUVA /
PRIMESL / MVC sources there - the legacy control_path call sites index
amdv_target_max[src_format][dst_format] raw and read out of bounds for those
formats. The helper refuses to reproduce that out-of-bounds read; fixing the
pre-existing OOB at those call sites is a separate patch.
 
2. THE MASTERING-DISPLAY PRIMARIES GO OUT ROTATED.
 
prepare_hdr10_param() normalises the source's g,b,r primaries into named
r_x/g_x/b_x fields, with its own comment saying it does so because "DV will swap
back to GBR in send_hdmi_pkt". That swap existed only on the HDR10+ branch; every
other source took a positional copy, so r,g,b went out where g,b,r was expected.
Measured, same title, same frame source:
 
  passthrough : (0.265,0.690) (0.150,0.060) (0.680,0.320)   = G,B,R  correct
  VS10 HDR10  : (0.680,0.320) (0.265,0.690) (0.150,0.060)   = R,G,B  rotated
 
The destination struct documents the contract the DV path was violating -
include/linux/amlogic/media/vout/vinfo.h:56 declares
 
	u32 primaries[3][2];		/* normalized 50000 in G,B,R order */
 
and the passthrough producer normalises to that order independently
(amcsc.c:4013-4023, "RGB -> GBR", rotation (i+2)%3) while hdmitx copies
positionally. Apply the swap for every source.
 
Expect NO picture change from this on a conforming sink: ANSI/CTA-861-H 6.9.1
requires sinks to recover the primary order geometrically ("The Red color
primary corresponds to the index of the largest display_primaries_x[] value"),
so a rotation is recoverable. It is fixed because the interface contract says
G,B,R and we were sending R,G,B - not because a symptom was traced to it. Do
not credit a picture change to this hunk.
 
--- a/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c	2026-08-06 16:39:14.029736086 -0400
+++ b/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c	2026-08-06 17:26:06.453755812 -0400
@@ -7007,6 +7007,41 @@
 /* #define HDMI_SEND_ALL_PKT */
 static u32 last_dst_format = FORMAT_SDR;
 
+/*
+ * The DM target actually handed to the control path for the current conversion,
+ * in the units the HDR10 mastering-display InfoFrame uses: max in nits, min in
+ * 0.0001 nit. On the multi path the committed setting carries it; otherwise the
+ * expression mirrors the legacy control_path call sites. Returns 0 when no
+ * target is known, in which case the caller leaves the source values alone.
+ */
+static u32 amdv_out_target_max_nits(enum signal_format_enum src_format,
+				    enum signal_format_enum dst_format)
+{
+	if (multi_dv_mode)
+		return m_dovi_setting.set_target_max_lum > 0 ?
+			(u32)m_dovi_setting.set_target_max_lum / 10000 : 0;
+
+	if (amdv_target_max_override)
+		return amdv_target_max_override;
+
+	if (src_format < FORMAT_DOVI ||
+	    src_format >= (int)ARRAY_SIZE(amdv_target_max) ||
+	    dst_format < FORMAT_DOVI ||
+	    dst_format >= (int)ARRAY_SIZE(amdv_target_max[0]))
+		return 0;
+
+	return amdv_target_max[src_format][dst_format];
+}
+
+static u32 amdv_out_target_min_lum(void)
+{
+	if (multi_dv_mode)
+		return m_dovi_setting.set_target_min_lum > 0 ?
+			(u32)m_dovi_setting.set_target_min_lum : 0;
+
+	return amdv_target_min_override ? amdv_target_min_override : amdv_target_min;
+}
+
 static bool send_hdmi_pkt
 	(enum signal_format_enum src_format,
 	 enum signal_format_enum dst_format,
